course: dictionary
lesson: 8976
title: Taking a sip
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sip can be a verb or a noun. As a verb, to sip means to drink slowly, taking only a small amount at a time. As a noun, a sip is a very small mouthful of a drink. Read the words around it to check which one you have: «After being sick, stay home and sip water» shows a verb, and «Sam took a sip of tea» shows a noun.
explain: The context often tells you why someone sips. «Shouting for a long time strains the vocal cords so the voice goes hoarse; resting it and sipping water usually bring it back.» Sipping is gentle. The water does not rush down the throat all at once, so the voice has time to rest and recover.
explain: Think about what the word does not mean. Sipping is not gulping, swallowing a whole glass in one go, or slurping. «Picture a sports day in July: the kids sipping water all morning are still running» shows that sipping is a steady, careful way of drinking, while the kids who forgot their bottles are the ones in trouble.
fact: Sip is a verb: to sip, sips, sipped, sipping.
fact: Sip is also a noun: a sip is a very small mouthful of a drink.
fact: «Sip» means to drink slowly, taking only a small amount at a time.
fact: We sip hot drinks so we do not burn our mouths.
fact: Sipping water helps when you feel sick, as «resting it and sipping water» shows for a hoarse voice.
exercise: «After being sick, stay home and sip water.» What does sip mean here? | Spill it on the floor | Pour it out of the bottle | *Drink slowly, a little at a time | Gulp it down all at once
exercise: Sam says, «She took a sip of tea.» What is a sip? | A loud shout across the playground | A big bowl of soup | *A very small mouthful of a drink | A spoon used for cooking
exercise: Why do we sip hot drinks? | To make them louder to drink | So they are easier to spill | So they get fizzy | *So we do not burn our mouths
test: «Shouting for a long time strains the vocal cords so the voice goes hoarse; resting it and sipping water usually bring it back.» What does sipping water mean? | Splashing it on your face | *Drinking slowly, a little at a time | Drinking it all at once | Gargling loudly
test: Which sentence uses sip as a noun? | He will sip his soup slowly | We sipped the juice at lunch | *She took a sip of warm milk | They are sipping cold lemonade
exam: Which sentence shows someone drinking slowly and carefully? | *She took a small sip of hot soup, blowing on it first | They tipped the bottle and swallowed everything | The bottle was emptied in a rush | He gulped the whole glass in one go
